Subject: [PROTOTYPE ONLY] winealsa,mmdevapi: add MIDI devices that appear after startup

alsa_midi_init() enumerates the sequencer once per process, so a
controller plugged in after an application started was invisible to it
until it restarted (issue 46). Patch 0028 re-points an existing table
entry at a returning device but cannot create one, because port_add()
reallocs the srcs/dests arrays and callers hold entry pointers across
the sequencer lock.

Reserve slots past the startup enumeration and freeze the tables, so a
runtime add writes into a stable slot and publishes it with a release
store: a reader without the lock sees either the old count or a fully
built entry. handle_port_start() then adds a port whose display name
matches nothing in the table instead of ignoring it, and the new
handle_port_exit() invalidates the address of a port that went away
while keeping its entry, so device ids stay stable and the existing
re-match by name still works when it returns.

Ports belonging to another Wine process's own sequencer client are
skipped: they are not devices, and adding them would fill every
process's list with entries that come and go as unrelated applications
start and stop.

Finally, tell the process the list moved. The unixlib posts a
device_change notify through the notify thread that already exists for
MIDI callbacks, and mmdevapi turns it into a WM_DEVICECHANGE
DBT_DEVNODES_CHANGED broadcast. That is the only device-change
notification that can be broadcast as-is: it needs no registration and
its lparam is 0, where an interface arrival carries a pointer that must
be valid in the receiving process.
